e**: CoCoHaNeRe [2025] (gradient-based > static embedding), ANCE [Xiong et al., 2021] (async index refresh). Paper claims to differ by controlling for methodology confounds.\n2. **Structure-aware code retrieval**: LocAgent [2024], RANGER [2024], CodexGraph [2024] (structural graphs at inference). ARISE [2024] (intra-procedural data-flow at runtime). Paper uses inter-procedural at training time via CodeQL.\n3. **Evaluation methodology in IR**: Zobel [1998] (pool depth, metric sensitivity). Paper extends to code retrieval.\n\n### Method (Section 3)\n- Task: SWE-smith, 14,100 instances, 131 repos\n- Training: LoRA, InfoNCE, K=7, τ=0.1\n- 4 strategies: Random, Embedding-distance (static), BM25, Dataflow-aware (CodeQL 2-hop, 34% coverage)\n- 3 encoders with distinct geometry\n- Evaluation: R@1 with explicit pool size\n\n### Experiments (Sections 4.1-4.13)\nExtensive experiments: main 9-run results for 3 encoders, BM25 baseline, methodology confound decomposition, pool size sensitivity, checkpoint criterion effect, temperature ablation, data scaling, dynamic mining, RepoBench-R cross-file eval, stratified analysis, statistical summary with bootstrap CIs, hop depth and fusion ablations.\n\n### Discussion (Section 5)\n- 5.1: Why methodology dominates (pool size creates \"criterion mismatch\")\n- 5.2: Geometry explains encoder-specificity (Qwen saturated → harder negatives help; StarCoder healthy margin → random already informative)\n- 5.3: Implications + 4 recommendations for the field\n- 5.4: Three limitations (3 seeds underpowered, only 3B/125M scale, dataflow 34% coverage)\n\n### Conclusion\nMethodology choices dominate mining effects 40×. Recommends standardized evaluation protocols.\n\n### References\n8 references total: CoCoHaNeRe [2025], LocAgent [2024], RANGER [2024], CodexGraph [2024], ARISE [2024], Xiong et al. [2021], Zobel [1998], SWE-smith [2025].\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the central contribution (methodology > mining) novel and significant? Is this an obvious finding dressed up, or a genuine insight?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are key prior works covered? What's missing?\n3. **Positioning**: Does the paper fairly represent prior work? Is the \"contradictory findings\" framing accurate?\n4. **Contribution significance**: Would this change how the community does research? Are the recommendations actionable?\n5. **Framing and narrative**: Is pivoting from \"mining helps\" to \"methodology dominates\" intellectually honest given the data?\n6. **Scope and generalizability**: How well do findings generalize beyond this specific setup?\n\nConsider these aspects:\n- The related work section is quite thin (3 short paragraphs, 8 references). For a methodology paper, this is notably sparse.\n- The paper references \"contradictory findings\" in the literature but doesn't actually cite specific contradictions\n- The \"40× ratio\" framing compares apples to oranges (methodology range vs strategy delta)\n- The geometry hypothesis (saturation explains encoder-specificity) is interesting but not rigorously tested\n- The recommendations in 5.3 are sensible but somewhat obvious (report at realistic pool sizes, use multiple seeds, etc.)\n- Is quantifying well-known confounds a sufficient contribution for a top venue?\n- The paper only tests on code retrieval — how well does this generalize to NL retrieval?\n\n## Output Format\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, 4-6 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, 4-6 items]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list, 3-5 items]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Overall Assessment\nScore: [1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept]\nConfidence: [1-5: 1=low, 5=expert]\n[2-3 sentence justification]\n```\n","uuid":"c563ad00-b316-410c-86b1-8d17a66154d9","session_id":"70f2e657-b2f7-49f6-b49a-3e4df9432c39"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue paper review. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. Review the paper thoroughly and produce a structured review.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Evaluation Methodology Dominates Mining Strategy: A Systematic Study of Confounds in Contrastive Code Retrieval\"\n\nThe paper compares 4 negative mining strategies (random, embedding-distance, BM25, dataflow-aware) across 3 encoder architectures (Qwen2.5-Coder-3B, StarCoder2-3B, UniXcoder-base 125M) for contrastive code retrieval trained on SWE-smith (14,100 instances, 131 repos). The central claim is that evaluation methodology choices (pool size, checkpoint criterion, temperature, seed, data size) account for 10-16pp variation in R@1, while the best mining strategy contributes at most 0.4pp — a 40× ratio.\n\n## Paper Structure\n\n### Abstract\nHard negative mining is widely assumed to be key for code retrieval. This paper conducts controlled study of 4 strategies × 3 encoders. Central finding: evaluation methodology accounts for 10-16pp variation, mining strategy ≤0.4pp (40× ratio). Embedding mining marginal on Qwen only (+0.40pp, CI includes zero). Proposes standardized evaluation recommendations.\n\n### Introduction\n- Repository-level code retrieval is foundational for automated SE\n- Contrastive learning with hard negative mining is dominant paradigm\n- Prior work: CoCoHaNeRe (gradient-based), LocAgent (graph-guided), ARISE (structure-aware)\n- Field has contradictory findings → hypothesis: caused by unstandardized evaluation methodology\n- Three contributions: (1) methodology dominates 40×, (2) mining effect is encoder-specific, (3) geometry analysis explains encoder-specificity\n\n### Related Work (Section 2)\nThree paragraphs:\n1. **Hard negative mining for code**: CoCoHaNeRe [2025] (gradient-based > static embedding), ANCE [Xiong et al., 2021] (async index refresh). Paper claims to differ by controlling for methodology confounds.\n2. **Structure-aware code retrieval**: LocAgent [2024], RANGER [2024], CodexGraph [2024] (structural graphs at inference). ARISE [2024] (intra-procedural data-flow at runtime). Paper uses inter-procedural at training time via CodeQL.\n3. **Evaluation methodology in IR**: Zobel [1998] (pool depth, metric sensitivity). Paper extends to code retrieval.\n\n### Method (Section 3)\n- Task: SWE-smith, 14,100 instances, 131 repos\n- Training: LoRA, InfoNCE, K=7, τ=0.1\n- 4 strategies: Random, Embedding-distance (static), BM25, Dataflow-aware (CodeQL 2-hop, 34% coverage)\n- 3 encoders with distinct geometry\n- Evaluation: R@1 with explicit pool size\n\n### Experiments (Sections 4.1-4.13)\nExtensive experiments: main 9-run results for 3 encoders, BM25 baseline, methodology confound decomposition, pool size sensitivity, checkpoint criterion effect, temperature ablation, data scaling, dynamic mining, RepoBench-R cross-file eval, stratified analysis, statistical summary with bootstrap CIs, hop depth and fusion ablations.\n\n### Discussion (Section 5)\n- 5.1: Why methodology dominates (pool size creates \"criterion mismatch\")\n- 5.2: Geometry explains encoder-specificity (Qwen saturated → harder negatives help; StarCoder healthy margin → random already informative)\n- 5.3: Implications + 4 recommendations for the field\n- 5.4: Three limitations (3 seeds underpowered, only 3B/125M scale, dataflow 34% coverage)\n\n### Conclusion\nMethodology choices dominate mining effects 40×. Recommends standardized evaluation protocols.\n\n### References\n8 references total: CoCoHaNeRe [2025], LocAgent [2024], RANGER [2024], CodexGraph [2024], ARISE [2024], Xiong et al. [2021], Zobel [1998], SWE-smith [2025].\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the central contribution (methodology > mining) novel and significant? Is this an obvious finding dressed up, or a genuine insight?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are key prior works covered? What's missing?\n3. **Positioning**: Does the paper fairly represent prior work? Is the \"contradictory findings\" framing accurate?\n4. **Contribution significance**: Would this change how the community does research? Are the recommendations actionable?\n5. **Framing and narrative**: Is pivoting from \"mining helps\" to \"methodology dominates\" intellectually honest given the data?\n6. **Scope and generalizability**: How well do findings generalize beyond this specific setup?\n\nConsider these aspects:\n- The related work section is quite thin (3 short paragraphs, 8 references). For a methodology paper, this is notably sparse.\n- The paper references \"contradictory findings\" in the literature but doesn't actually cite specific contradictions\n- The \"40× ratio\" framing compares apples to oranges (methodology range vs strategy delta)\n- The geometry hypothesis (saturation explains encoder-specificity) is interesting but not rigorously tested\n- The recommendations in 5.3 are sensible but somewhat obvious (report at realistic pool sizes, use multiple seeds, etc.)\n- Is quantifying well-known confounds a sufficient contribution for a top venue?\n- The paper only tests on code retrieval — how well does this generalize to NL retrieval?\n\n## Output Format\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, 4-6 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, 4-6 items]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list, 3-5 items]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Overall Assessment\nScore: [1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept]\nConfidence: [1-5: 1=low, 5=expert]\n[2-3 sentence justification]\n```\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Qii4gNPr6dfrnDcaTLzJk5","session_id":"70f2e657-b2f7-49f6-b49a-3e4df9432c39","uuid":"e7468b5b-337e-48e7-8ca5-3b352f116037","timestamp":"2026-05-16T16:10:01.401Z"}
